m and we're  seeing the brunt of that in terms of  global energy costs going up this year  uh not least because um there has been  this epic under investment in the energy  sector which is partly cyclical because  obviously covid um amongst other things  so last you know in 2020 we saw march 20  i can't remember  around march 2020 we saw you know oil  prices going almost internet well they  did momentarily go into negative  territory which is not exactly a great  um sort of incentive to invest in the in  the energy sector so there's a cyclical  uh side to that story but on top of that  there is this stranded asset issue which  is that nobody wants to invest because  they fear that um the investments will  never get used and energy investing is  very long-term horizon focus so if  you're not going to get your money back  in say 20 30 years because uh by 2030 20  20 35 you know these assets can't be  utilized to the degree that you might  otherwise want them to so that's that's  where i'm looking at things um that's  the perspective i'm looking at things  from  thanks very much indeed isabella and  plenty there for us to uh to to chew  over um paulie could i now turn to polly  toynbee for the final pitch on this  question are current levels of inflation  putting us on the brink of economic  disaster over to you  i think we have to start from a position  of great humility i think that economics  itself is in a state of great crisis and  we are at the moment finding that we  know less and less and that fewer and  fewer of the old rules seem to hold um i  don't think we know what money is or how  it works and  uh the arrival of crypto currency for  instance has been quite a shock to the  system because it goes against all the  rules does it work does it not work is  it a terrifying dangerous explosive  element in our economies  we have a budget next week and our  chancellor rishi sunak sounds as if he's  going to cleave to all the old economic  conventional possibly untruths um a  fixed idea that um  spending must be covered by by taxation  that you cannot in any one year  um  allow the the deficit to rise we're not  sure that that's true uh or we're  certainly not sure that that medicine  isn't a great deal worse than the  disease it's trying to cure  um  we know the banks create money we've  seen qe has been an extraordinary way of  creating money creating huge amounts of  extra assets for the very wealthy  uh it hasn't been a very satisfactory  way of of dealing with the the the 20  uh  with the 2009 crisis  um  i think it turns out that there is a  kind of magic money tree but nobody  knows how tall it can grow how how how  long it can last  um  what's certain is that as long as stable  countries and we've just had professor  hanky talking about very extreme  collapse societies collapse politics  collapsed economies which i don't think  are very relevant for the relatively  solid economies of our own countries um  we don't you know we can borrow as much  as lenders are willing to lend at the  moment they seem very willing to lend  because they don't know where else to  put their money  so there doesn't seem to be as much  difficulty in um constantly replenishing  the magic money tree i think any idea of  return to that old monetarism  would be a serious a serious error i  think raising interest rates now would  be catastrophic um we know immediately  that how how it's how bad its effects  will be but we don't know what not  raising interest rates might do and  whether how bad its effect might be and  if you know one thing and not the other  then you should use the precautionary  principle and you should not raise  interest rates now it doesn't appear  that wages are driving uh its prices are  raw materials and supplies um not wages  wages are not rising in in written  significantly well maybe for a few hgv  drivers but not for anybody else much  certainly not rising above the rate of  inflation okay  so let's not repeat the big errors that  this country made in 2010 when by uh  imposing strict austerity uh  under george osborne and david cameron  uh turned off the taps it meant that we  were slowest to recover uh from the uh  the great financial crash  uh the great austerity did terrible harm  socially and economically and we have  been behind and we were least prepared  as a result  for the covet crisis we were more  vulnerable than other equivalent  countries  so let's uh hold on to the  doctors  the doctor's principle first do no harm  let's not make things worse by turning  off the taps now  to continue watching this video click  the link in the top left or in the  description below or visit iai.tv for  more debates and talks from the world's  leading thinkers on today's biggest  ideas

